    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 211805\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1104 AM PDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Tornado Watch and Flash Flood Watch in effect for Mohave County, \n  AZ today. Strong to severe storms expected for NW Arizona and also \n  the lower Great Basin in Inyo, Esmeralda, and Nye Counties. \n  Damaging winds, large hail, isolated tornadoes, and heavy rain are \n  all threats.\n\n* Additional rounds of rain and storms expected across much of the \n  region tonight and Thursday with mainly a heavy rain, flash flood, \n  and lightning threat. \n  \n* Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures expected this weekend \n  and early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through next Tuesday.\n\n.TODAY'S SEVERE WEATHER THREAT FOR NW ARIZONA, FAR EAST NEVADA, AND \nALSO THE LOWER GREAT BASIN.\n\nActive weather is already ongoing this morning across the region \nwith numerous showers and thunderstorms in NW Arizona and along the \nNV/UT border. Important note for the day, Las Vegas the Colorado \nRiver Valley and portions of the Mohave Desert in San Bernardino \nCounty will likely NOT be impacted by today's severe weather threat, \nbut will update the forecast if needed. Greatest area of concern for \nsevere weather will be over Northern and Eastern portions of Mohave \nCounty AZ, then a secondary area to watch over Inyo, Esmeralda, and \nNye counties. Today's set up includes a strong upper low off the \ncoast of California while a 90-100kt jet streak in AZ places the \nregion in an area of strong upper level diffluence and enhanced lift \nunder the left exit region. Effective bulk shear of 45-60 kt and \nincreasing instability will be more than sufficient for discrete \ncells and supercell development later this afternoon capable of \nproducing large hail, damaging winds, and even isolated tornadoes. \nLong hodographs indicate the potential for splitting supercells and \nleft movers today which could produce hail up to 2\" in diameter. \nAnonymously low LCLs across Mohave County in addition to 200-300 \nm2/s2 effective SR helicity will aid in the localized tornado threat \nin the strongest storms. Supercell composite above 4 and SigTor \nparameter above 1 confirm the primed atmosphere for a severe weather \nevent not typically seen in this part of the country. Further west \nover the higher terrain of Inyo County and far western Nevada, \nparameters are similar however the tornado threat is significantly \nlower there. Organized thunderstorms will be possible further west \nwith damaging winds from downbursts, hail, heavy rain, and lightning \nas threats. PWATs between 1-1.35\" should allow for fairly efficient \nand heavy rainfall under any storm, but due to the fast storm motion \ntoday, flash flooding will be confined to areas where storms train \nover the same area for a period of time. Greatest threat for Flash \nFlooding will be in Mohave County where more widespread convection \nis already ongoing and training storms are more likely.\n\n\nTiming: Ongoing storms this morning in Mohave County, but the \ngreatest threat for severe weather will be Noon to 7 PM. Expecting \nstorms to begin developing further west toward Inyo County after \n12pm-1pm. Potential is there for showers and storms to continue into \nthe late evening and nighttime hours with a lessening severe weather \nafter sunset.\n\n\n.Thursday through Early Next Week.\n\nThe primary vort max of the low/trough swings through on Thursday, \nbringing the best chance of widespread precipitation to our \nregion. PoPs on Thursday are 50% or more in most locations, though\nthe southern Mojave Desert remains the greatest area of \"bust\" \npotential with PoPs 10-40%. Precipitation chances largely exit the\nCWA Thursday night once the shortwave trough axis clears us, but \na secondary shortwave diving south will keep the larger trough \noverhead on Friday. This allows shower chances (20-40%) to linger,\nprimarily in the southern Great Basin. The trough finally pushes \neast on Saturday, although guidance has trended slower with this. \nThe weekend should be much drier, but if the trough continues to \nslow, low precipitation chances may linger in our eastern areas on\nSaturday. \n\nThe hydro/convective concerns are going to be the primary focus\nwith this system, but there are other aspects worth mentioning \nas well. Snow levels bottom out around 8kft, but most of the \nprecip will fall while they are 9kft+, thus limiting any winter \nimpacts to the higher elevations of the Sierra. Gusty winds will \nbe present as well with this storm, with widespread southerly \ngusts of 20-40 mph today and Thursday. Friday into Saturday, we \nsee a shift to gusty north winds as the system pulls away. \nTemperatures will be near normal today, dropping to below-normal \nvalues tomorrow through Saturday. Dry and seasonable conditions \nexpected Sunday through Tuesday as we see a lull in the storm \ntrack.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nLight southeast winds will gradually increase later this morning and \nearly afternoon, with speeds 10-15KT and gusts to around 20-25KT \nexpected through sunset. Winds become light and somewhat variable \novernight, increasing out of the southwest toward the end of the \nforecast period. Clouds will be SCT-BKN around 8-12kft through the \nforecast period, with some improvement expected this afternoon. \nThunderstorm activity is expected to generally remain north and east \nof the Las Vegas Valley, impacts to approaches can be expected due \nto scattered to numerous thunderstorms across the Mormon Mesa and \nPeach Springs Corridors this afternoon onward.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Showers and \nthunderstorms will become more widespread this afternoon across the \nsouthern Great Basin, northwestern Arizona, and portions of \nsoutheastern California. Convection is largely expected to stay out \nof the vicinity of area terminals, with the exception of KBIH, where \nat least vicinity showers and thunderstorms are expected through the \nforecast period. The best window for thunderstorm impacts at KBIH \nremains between around 21/20Z-22/02Z, with erratic gusty winds, \nlightning strikes, and ceiling/visibility reductions due to rainfall \nexpected. Cloud bases will generally be at or above 10kft, but will \nbe as low as 6-8kft in the vicinity of convection. Outside of any \nthunderstorm influences, south to southeast winds 10-15KT with gusts \n20-30KT will be fairly common. Winds will diminish after sunset. &&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore/Woods \nAVIATION...Gorelow/Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
